<p>Israel’s Knesset approved a law late Monday establishing a special military tribunal in Jerusalem to try Palestinians accused of taking part in the Hamas-led October 7, 2023, attack, with authority to impose the death penalty in the gravest cases. The measure passed 93-0 in the 120-seat parliament, with the remaining lawmakers absent or abstaining, and drew support from members of the governing coalition and the opposition. A three-judge panel will preside over the court, which could try an estimated 200 to 300 Palestinians captured in Israel during the attack, as well as others later detained in Gaza and suspected of participating in the assault or of holding or abusing Israeli hostages. Israeli authorities say the October 7 attack killed at least 1,200 people, most of them civilians, and resulted in 251 hostages being taken to Gaza; Gaza health authorities say Israel’s subsequent assault killed more than 72,000 Palestinians, mostly civilians, and left much of Gaza in ruins. No trial date has been set.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ncwMLKIP8-MfFtUufjgFMH5UWTdCpkhXGNcesWKERpGT-LirIFyUeiRQa60YElaCCJpq64ldLHSwfpynSrxwVTXIlIU2avbWNVznLPFMcw' alt='Hand prints and other markings made in soot on a wall nearly a year after the October 7 attack in Kibbutz Beeri, southern Israel.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Bill sponsors: Religious Zionism lawmaker Simcha Rothman and Yisrael Beiteinu lawmaker Yulia Malinovsky introduced the bill.</li><li>Public proceedings: Major hearings will be public and broadcast live, with video and audio recordings kept in state archives or posted through a designated website.</li><li>Possible charges: Charges may include crimes against the Jewish people, crimes against humanity and war crimes, while some sources also reported genocide-related offenses under the new framework.</li><li>Legislative timing: The vote came as the Knesset opened its summer session after a spring recess of more than a month.</li></ul><blockquote>Out of the terrible massacre, we rose up to fulfill our moral obligation to bring the perpetrators to justice. - Israeli Justice Minister Yariv Levin</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Knesset member Yulia Malinovsky: She said the proceedings would be trials of “modern-day Nazis” and would enter the history books. (<a href='https://www.birgun.net/haber/israil-meclisi-nden-idam-onayi-aksa-tufani-operasyonuna-karistigi-one-surulen-filistinliler-ozel-mahkemede-yargilanacak-711851'>BirGün</a>)</li><li>Knesset member Simcha Rothman: Rothman, a Religious Zionism lawmaker, said the law sends a message that Israel “will not forget and will not forgive.” (<a href='https://www.jns.org/news/israel-news/israel-approves-special-tribunal-to-prosecute-oct-7-terrorists-authorizes-death-penalty'>JNS</a>)</li><li>Yale Law School expert Ya’ara Mordecai: Mordecai said the law raised due-process concerns because of the military court setting and the risk that atrocity proceedings could become politicized or symbolic “show trials.” (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1999623/new-israeli-law-sets-military-tribunal-for-hamas-fighters-linked-to-october-7-attack'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Adalah lawyer Mouna Haddad: Haddad said the legislation lowers fair-trial guarantees and permits mass proceedings that can depart from ordinary evidentiary rules, including broader admission of evidence allegedly obtained under coercion. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/5/12/israel-approves-law-on-public-trials-death-penalty-for-october-7-detainees'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3353236/israel-pushes-ahead-eichmann-style-tribunal-october-7-attackers'>Israel pushes ahead with Eichmann-style tribunal for October 7 attackers</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/israeli-lawmakers-set-special-tribunal-death-penalty-hamas-132862937'>Israeli lawmakers set up a special tribunal and allow for de